To: Executive Leadership Team, Supply Chain and Sustainability
From: Sustainability & Logistics Director
Subject: Balanced shipping plan to manage cost and carbon under real-world constraints
Date: 

Context
We are updating our outbound transportation plan to balance two outcomes that frequently pull in opposite directions: keeping delivered cost competitive while reducing our carbon footprint. Each shipping lane between a warehouse and a store carries both a per‑unit monetary cost and a per‑unit emissions rate. In practice, the lowest‑cost lanes often carry higher emissions, so we must make deliberate trade‑offs rather than pursuing cost alone.

Operational rules we must respect
- Route activation carries a one‑time setup expense. Opening a lane triggers contract and administrative fees, dock preparation, and onboarding; if a lane is not activated, it cannot be used and does not incur this expense.
- Activated lanes require a minimum shipment size. To avoid underutilized equipment and handling inefficiencies, partial loads are not permitted; once a lane is open, we must commit to at least a defined minimum order quantity on that lane.
- Store‑level supplier limits apply. To manage receiving capacity and vendor complexity, each store can be served by only a limited number of distinct warehouses; any plan must respect that cap.

Capacity and service requirements
- Shipments out of each warehouse cannot exceed the inventory available for distribution.
- Each store’s minimum demand must be fully covered within the planning horizon.
- Any activated lane must carry at least its required minimum volume.
- No store may receive from more than the allowed number of warehouses.

Management priority
Leadership has set explicit importance weights for cost and emissions. We will use these weights to blend the variable portion of shipping spend and the total emissions across all lanes. The one‑time setup costs for any activated lanes will be added in full on top of that blended total; they are not scaled by the weights. This ensures we reflect both our financial and sustainability priorities while accounting for the real activation expenses of opening lanes.

Business goal
Identify which lanes to activate and how much to ship on each lane so that:
- Warehouse capacity is respected and store demand is met without shortfalls,
- Activated lanes meet their minimum shipment commitments,
- The number of supplying warehouses per store stays within the allowed limit, and
- The overall total—comprised of the importance‑weighted variable shipping spend and emissions, plus the full setup costs for any activated lanes—is as low as possible.
The resulting plan will present a clear set of activated lanes with shipment quantities that balance cost and carbon outcomes while honoring the rules above. We will also be able to run scenario tests that adjust the relative importance of cost and emissions to understand trade‑offs before final sign‑off.

